**Pick a simple domain** (for example `Account` or `Employee`), say what operations you will support (create and get by id), and describe each layer's job before writing code.

- **Entity:** a plain model mapped to a table, with an id and fields, no business logic about other layers.
- **DAO / Repository:** the only layer that talks to the database; returns entities or `Optional`.
- **Service:** business rules, validation, transaction boundary, converts between DTOs and entities.
- **Controller:** HTTP only: parse the request, call the service, map results to status codes.

    @Entity class Account { @Id Long id; String owner; BigDecimal balance; /* getters */ }

    interface AccountDao { Optional<Account> findById(Long id); Account save(Account a); }

    @Service class AccountService {
      private final AccountDao dao;
      AccountService(AccountDao dao) { this.dao = dao; }
      @Transactional
      Account create(CreateAccountRequest r) {
        if (r.owner() == null || r.owner().isBlank()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("owner required");
        return dao.save(new Account(r.owner(), BigDecimal.ZERO));
      }
      Account get(Long id) { return dao.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException(id)); }
    }

    @RestController @RequestMapping("/accounts") class AccountController {
      private final AccountService service;
      AccountController(AccountService s) { this.service = s; }
      @GetMapping("/{id}") AccountDto get(@PathVariable Long id) { return AccountDto.from(service.get(id)); }
      @PostMapping ResponseEntity<AccountDto> create(@RequestBody CreateAccountRequest r) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(AccountDto.from(service.create(r)));
      }
    }

**Points to say aloud:** constructor injection and depending on interfaces (testable with mocks); DTOs keep entities out of the API; a global `@ControllerAdvice` maps `NotFoundException` to 404 and validation errors to 400; transactions belong in the service, not the DAO or controller; `BigDecimal` for money.

**Avoid:** business logic in the controller, controllers calling the DAO directly, exposing entities, swallowing exceptions, and no input validation.
